WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.573 --> 00:00:04.120
("Rhapsody in Blue")

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.540
- [Daniel] Chapter one:
he adored Las Vegas—

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.927
a city that looked as beautiful as him.

00:00:09.927 --> 00:00:12.430
No, no, no, no, that's too narcissistic.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:14.000
Let me start this over.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.130
To him, Las Vegas was a city full of

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:19.140
color, energy and opportunity.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.440
A complete contrast
from where he came from.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.540
This is the story of
how Daniel Pohl escaped

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.510
the clutches of the Iron Curtain

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:31.890
on a zip line and won his freedom.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.650
Yeah, this sounds much better.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.650
(zip line whirring)

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.780
- [Narrator Two] Hold on,
let's pause for a second.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.205
To understand this story,
we need to go back.

00:00:44.205 --> 00:00:45.038
(film reel stuttering)

00:00:45.038 --> 00:00:48.180
Like way back, like back to the '80s.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.580
The place is Czechoslovakia,
the ruling party—Communist.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:54.540
Our character is Daniel Pohl,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.490
a young man who was politically outspoken.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.737
- People that knew me
really well, they told me,

00:00:59.737 --> 00:01:01.880
"Hey listen, all I can tell you is,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.490
“you really want to tone it
down on your political speech

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.100
“because you may end up in prison."

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.480
And so that's when I
starting plotting escape.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.530
- [Narrator] While
planning his exit strategy,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.130
Daniel met another radical thinker,

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.070
a physicist named Robert Ospald.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.380
- Robert was pretty well
gifted when it came to physics.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.880
He was imprisoned for political reasons,

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.558
so I knew that this guy is crazy enough

00:01:22.558 --> 00:01:25.760
to be my partner in crime to get out with.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.340
We tried to come up with all
kinds of crazy ideas, like

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.370
building our own rocket and
flying over the Iron Curtain.

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.400
Digging tunnels, none of it
seemed viable for many reasons.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.790
Then one day, I see Robert
glancing at a power line

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.990
and something told me: “You
should be worried.” (laughing)

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:45.980
He says, "We're gonna
zip line into Austria."

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.170
I said, "No, dude, you're
clinically insane."

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.040
And he says, "And that's
why we're gonna get out."

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.800
- [Narrator] So the two got to work,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.590
and over the next several months

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.810
they built—and tested—their own pulleys.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:03.000
- The plan was to
infiltrate the border zone,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.600
climb up the tower, set up the pulleys,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.590
zip line over the Iron Curtain,

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.250
climb down from the next
tower into no mans land,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.402
and then make it into Austria.

00:02:13.402 --> 00:02:14.235
- (Robert) Woo hoo.

00:02:14.235 --> 00:02:16.240
- The reason why this was
so insanely dangerous,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.120
because we could have
gotten shot at the border.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.740
Could have gotten electrocuted.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.920
Could have gotten torn
apart by border dogs.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.620
And then you have 380,000 volts

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.430
coursing underneath your feet.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.120
Yeah, this mission was, to put it

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.820
mildly, extremely dangerous.

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.100
It was almost like a “Mission Impossible.”

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:35.860
- [Narrator] But that
didn't stop these two.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:39.030
With their pulley's made,
and their plan in place,

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.550
they headed out and climbed the tower.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.150
- Robert went first, and when he

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.600
took off, my heart just sunk.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.960
And I was like, yeah, there is

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.180
no turning back, I am doing this.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.070
I just decided to go
for it, I went for it.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.320
When I was zip lining,
it was like a mixture of

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.190
emotions you never experienced before.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.540
You are way up high, you have a lot of

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.210
electricity around you, it was crazy.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:06.320
But, despite all the
close calls, we made it.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.440
- [Narrator] Daniel and Robert
became local celebrities,

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.030
appearing in news outlets all over Europe.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.970
Daniel even donated his pulley to the

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.270
Checkpoint Charlie museum in Berlin.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.700
The pulley still hangs there today.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.480
Robert remained in Austria, but Daniel,

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.640
his sights were on America.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.480
So he moved to the one place that

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.380
could match his personality—Vegas.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.530
So I wrote a book, I got
married, drive a cab in Vegas.

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:33.080
I don't think I would
drive a cab anywhere else,

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.310
but in Vegas, it's fun.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:38.163
Very international city,
very dynamic—it's awesome.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.330
I wanted to be free at all costs.

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.390
Yes, I wanted to piss off the communists.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.970
I wanted to show them
that it doesn't matter

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:47.010
what Iron Curtain, what
wall they're gonna build,

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.850
how many border patrol
guards they're gonna place

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.270
on the border, I'm still getting through.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.020
They can not defeat the human spirit

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.120
that wants its freedom.

00:03:57.054 --> 00:03:58.938
That was awesome.

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:01.938
(upbeat jazz music)

00:04:02.931 --> 00:04:05.514
(bell dinging)

